O.k. Not sure why this is getting confused, so let me try it this way:

"Usually tolerated by compilers, "

...means pretty much exactly what it says. It's a non-printable
character that the compiler may or may not ignore. Use at your own risk.

"...but often considered bad form."

...means pretty much exactly what it says. It may or may not produce
desirable or "pretty" results, since most compilers keep their own line
counters and such for formatting listings. Yes, I did see one that
output the <FF> transparently to the listing file, but ignored it's
significance. The results ranged from amusing to aggravating.

"Not recommended."

Admittedly, a statement of opinion. Should be self-explanatory, but if
you need help with it, post your questions here.

>   The discussion of file transfers over to Microsoft Windows systems almost
>   certainly involves a need to switch the sequential files to Stream or to
>   Stream LF format -- before moving the files along to the Windows platforms.
>   OpenVMS has a number of sequential file formats and record organizations,
>   and only a select few combinations are compatible with the Microsoft
>   sequential file formats and expectations.

Note also that I've seen cases where various format conversion software
(FTP, CONVERT, ZIP/UNZIP, etc.) tended treat <FF> identically to 
<CR><LF> or <LF>: as a record terminator. Never did ferret out the
cause.

Some DCL proc.'s also use <FF> for formatting ... which is weird because
some of those same proc.'s may also have escape characters embedded in
literal strings instead of using symbols or substitution. So, the
proc.'s aren't "printable", anyway.

