In article <bo3lsu$p3s$07$1@news.t-online.com>, "Kaya Memisoglu" <k.memisoglu@majix.de> writes:
:>   Both the AlphaServer 1000 and the AlphaServer 1000A series EV4-class
:>   processors provide four banks of five SIMMs each.  Four SIMMs in the
:>   bank provide data storage, and the fifth provides the ECC coverage.
:There is one thing I just don't understand: Why does the Alphaserver EV4
:need five SIMMs of ECC memory for ECC correction. It is obvious that
:four ECC modules would be enough for error correction. Is this a bad
:design of the Alphaservers? Are the ECC bits of the SIMMs used at
:all in an EV4 configuration? Is this a dirty hack?


  The EV4 processor uses a longword ECC scheme across the 36-bit SIMMs,
  while the EV5 processor uses quadword ECC and the same 36-bit SIMMs --
  the latter approach requires fewer bits for ECC storage, and thus one
  fewer SIMM is needed within each memory bank present on the EV5-based
  AlphaServer 1000 and AlphaServer 1000A series configurations; four
  SIMMs per bank, rather than the five SIMMs required per bank on the 
  EV4-based systems.

  A related note from one of the manuals that was associated with this 
  particular change:

    "NOTE: A change in the ECC (error correction code) from
    longword to quadword allows the code to reside on the
    memory modules, eliminating the need for ECC modules on
    AlphaServer 1000/1000A Model 5/xxx systems"

  The SIMMs used are the same for both the EV4 and EV5 processors; the
  original ECC design for the platform had anticipated that this change
  might become possible.

