My guess is the following as indicated in the other post: With
EV4 boards, there is an error correction of 1 bit per 32bits of
memory. This requires at least 38bits if I am not mistaken, so
36bit modules are just not enough for this type of ECC.

In an EV5 configuration, the board will correct 1 error in four
parallel memory modules, that is 1 error in 128bit. This should
require at least 9 additional bits, that is a total of 137bits and
using four 36bit ECC memory modules they have 4x36bits = 144bits.

So the difference is that in a 5 module configuration, they can
correct one error in every 32bit word, while in a 4 module
configuration they can only correct 1 error in a 128bit quad
word. (I am not sure how many additinal bits are needed for
detecting one additional error without correction)

But of course, this is just some theory, I really don't know
how it really works. But these guesses are built upon the information
in the posting of Hoff Hoffman in this thread.

Kaya




